1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,160
And now for you, Scoutmaster.

2
00:00:08,160 --> 00:00:11,380
There was a story in the news recently about an 8-year-old Cub Scout

3
00:00:11,380 --> 00:00:16,520
who shares the name of somebody who is on a TSA watch list.

4
00:00:17,760 --> 00:00:20,220
And the boy went to get on a commercial air flight

5
00:00:20,220 --> 00:00:25,320
and caused a little bit of stir amongst the transportation security people.

6
00:00:25,320 --> 00:00:31,880
And there was a little bit on the NPR show,

7
00:00:32,040 --> 00:00:35,500
Wait, Wait, Don't Tell Me, about this particular incident.

8
00:00:35,880 --> 00:00:37,920
And, hey, it's Scout-related.

9
00:00:38,360 --> 00:00:39,040
I thought it was funny.

10
00:00:39,900 --> 00:00:41,660
It really is.

11
00:00:41,720 --> 00:00:45,980
It's maddening because the TSA is spending all this time on this Cub Scout

12
00:00:45,980 --> 00:00:50,100
when Weebelos and Brownies are just gallivanting right through security.

13
00:00:50,380 --> 00:00:50,540
Terrible.

14
00:00:50,940 --> 00:00:52,480
They need to start profiling.

15
00:00:53,180 --> 00:00:55,180
Anybody with a kerchief gets pulled off.

16
00:00:55,180 --> 00:00:56,060
I mean, it's the...

17
00:00:56,060 --> 00:00:57,980
Weebelos sounds like some call to arm.

18
00:00:57,980 --> 00:01:00,400
Weebelos wobble, but they never fall down.

19
00:01:01,840 --> 00:01:03,120
Weebelo, weebelo, weebelo.

20
00:01:03,200 --> 00:01:04,760
It sounds like some call to war.

21
00:01:07,680 --> 00:01:08,860
So this is Clark Green.

22
00:01:08,940 --> 00:01:10,520
Welcome to the Scoutmasters podcast.

23
00:01:10,520 --> 00:01:14,440
This is podcast number four.

24
00:01:18,920 --> 00:01:19,960
Hey!

25
00:01:25,180 --> 00:01:49,260
Scoutmastership in seven minutes.

26
00:01:49,760 --> 00:01:51,120
Or less.

27
00:01:51,120 --> 00:01:57,780
Hey, everybody.

28
00:01:57,860 --> 00:01:58,140
How you doing?

29
00:01:58,220 --> 00:02:06,480
This came into the email inbox this week from Mark Bowie, Scoutmaster of Troop 531 in Orange, California.

30
00:02:07,760 --> 00:02:08,360
He says,

31
00:02:08,460 --> 00:02:09,440
Hello, Clark.

32
00:02:09,600 --> 00:02:11,800
I enjoy your thoughtful blogs and podcasts.

33
00:02:11,880 --> 00:02:12,540
Well, thank you, Mark.

34
00:02:13,300 --> 00:02:16,580
And I'm interested to learn about your views on Merit Badge Days.

35
00:02:16,580 --> 00:02:29,460
Now, what I'm understanding is Merit Badge Day is kind of like a Merit Badge College or any kind of an event where multiple merit badges are offered with counselors attached.

36
00:02:29,460 --> 00:02:36,200
I know our local museum near us offers two or three merit badges a couple, three days a year.

37
00:02:36,720 --> 00:02:39,740
And Scouts can come in, and as a group, they can work their way through the Merit Badge.

38
00:02:42,240 --> 00:02:42,880
He says,

39
00:02:42,880 --> 00:02:48,680
Your podcast item about Baden Powell's view on badge hunters versus badge earners was timely.

40
00:02:49,460 --> 00:02:53,220
And we have recently been having a debate in our troop about Merit Badge Days.

41
00:02:53,960 --> 00:02:57,920
One side says that the primary purpose is to raise money for the hosting troop.

42
00:02:58,799 --> 00:03:07,019
And I don't really know how that works, but I'll accept the fact that somewhere out there a troop is hosting a Merit Badge Day and they might be doing it as a fundraiser.

43
00:03:07,020 --> 00:03:12,800
And not to ensure quality instruction for the boys and that they're only a shortcut on Merit Badge completion.

44
00:03:13,420 --> 00:03:20,820
Others say that in today's busy schedule, it's too hard to find a Merit Badge counselor and schedule meetings with them to review progress.

45
00:03:21,660 --> 00:03:26,560
And that Merit Badge Days are the only practical way outside of summer camp to complete Merit Badges.

46
00:03:26,620 --> 00:03:27,100
What do you think?

47
00:03:27,100 --> 00:03:31,600
Well, I'm going to stand directly in the middle.

48
00:03:32,900 --> 00:03:34,020
Because I agree.

49
00:03:34,400 --> 00:03:36,780
I agree with both sides to a certain extent.

50
00:03:37,020 --> 00:03:38,580
So let me explain myself.

51
00:03:39,080 --> 00:03:42,120
First of all, Merit Badges, there's a very simple system.

52
00:03:42,580 --> 00:03:46,780
And there's very simple rules about who gets involved where.

53
00:03:47,240 --> 00:03:50,480
The boy brings you a blank blue card, Mr. Scoutmaster.

54
00:03:50,880 --> 00:03:54,760
He says, I am going to do a camping Merit Badge.

55
00:03:55,020 --> 00:03:57,700
And I would ask, who is your counselor?

56
00:03:58,220 --> 00:04:00,100
And I would say, is he a certified counselor?

57
00:04:00,680 --> 00:04:06,860
And if I needed to check up on that, I would use what resources I needed to make sure that this was a registered Merit Badge counselor.

58
00:04:07,020 --> 00:04:16,100
And I would possibly review with the boy the youth protection specifications around Merit Badges.

59
00:04:16,200 --> 00:04:18,660
There are no one-on-one meetings with adults.

60
00:04:18,900 --> 00:04:22,500
In other words, bring a scout with you or there needs to be a second adult present.

61
00:04:22,500 --> 00:04:26,480
And then I would pat him on his head and send him on his happy way.

62
00:04:27,980 --> 00:04:32,040
Several days, weeks, months later, he will bring me a completed blue card.

63
00:04:32,040 --> 00:04:36,800
I will tell him to take it to the advancement chairman or the troop scribe.

64
00:04:37,240 --> 00:04:42,620
And then the troop scribe or the advancement chairman will hand me the blue card to sign at one point.

65
00:04:43,220 --> 00:04:54,100
And I will hand the Scout a Merit Badge at our earliest convenience and shake his hand and congratulate him for a job well done.

66
00:04:54,100 --> 00:05:03,600
Now, nowhere in there, nowhere in that very simple process, does the Scoutmaster interfere with who the counselor is,

67
00:05:04,280 --> 00:05:09,760
if it's a registered Merit Badge counselor, the rigor of the study of the Merit Badge,

68
00:05:09,860 --> 00:05:14,400
or the quality of the instruction of the Merit Badge, or the quality of work that a Scout does with the Merit Badge.

69
00:05:14,540 --> 00:05:15,960
I see only blue cards.

70
00:05:16,280 --> 00:05:17,280
I see happy Scouts.

71
00:05:17,440 --> 00:05:18,640
I hand out Merit Badges.

72
00:05:18,640 --> 00:05:27,760
If, for some reason, I was to become concerned about the instruction of a given Merit Badge,

73
00:05:27,940 --> 00:05:33,760
or I was to become concerned that, you know, somebody was just giving Merit Badges away,

74
00:05:34,780 --> 00:05:40,979
which is a concern that's often expressed, but in 25 years I have never, ever seen anybody giving Merit Badges away,

75
00:05:41,320 --> 00:05:46,080
then I would take that concern to the people who are certifying Merit Badge counselors.

76
00:05:46,080 --> 00:05:51,820
Because it is not the Scout's fault if the Merit Badge counselor is not doing his job.

77
00:05:51,900 --> 00:05:53,280
It's the Merit Badge counselor's fault.

78
00:05:54,400 --> 00:05:56,039
Who certifies Merit Badge counselors?

79
00:05:56,260 --> 00:06:00,320
Well, your Council Advancement Committee does, and your District Advancement Committee,

80
00:06:00,440 --> 00:06:02,539
and that would be the person I would take the concerns to.

81
00:06:02,680 --> 00:06:08,099
If you really think, if people are really thinking that these Merit Badge days, as you call them,

82
00:06:08,100 --> 00:06:18,100
are just shortcuts and take it to the next level and ask what kind of quality control is being done for the counselors that are operating there and things like that.

83
00:06:18,140 --> 00:06:23,300
Otherwise, keep your nose out and let the Scouts go and do and achieve.

84
00:06:24,160 --> 00:06:29,040
A Merit Badge is not a certificate of completion of a course of rigorous study, I've got to tell you.

85
00:06:29,040 --> 00:06:35,740
A Scout with a Life Saving Merit Badge is not a qualified Lifesaver, nor is a Scout with a First Aid Merit Badge an EMT.

86
00:06:35,860 --> 00:06:38,740
Those kind of certifications require far more rigorous study.

87
00:06:39,080 --> 00:06:43,360
First Aid Merit Badge and a Life Saving Merit Badge are valuable, and they're a valuable set of skills.

88
00:06:43,920 --> 00:06:51,080
But we would never depend on them as a sole indication of somebody's skill level, maturity,

89
00:06:51,280 --> 00:06:55,620
and their ability to handle a situation that a Life Guard or an EMT would handle, would we?

90
00:06:55,620 --> 00:07:01,160
Now, I am going to differ with the others that say, in today's busy schedules,

91
00:07:01,340 --> 00:07:04,500
it's too hard to find a Merit Badge counselor and schedule meetings and things like that.

92
00:07:04,560 --> 00:07:05,700
Well, we have things like email.

93
00:07:06,120 --> 00:07:10,800
I have done significant parts of Merit Badges that I counsel with email.

94
00:07:11,560 --> 00:07:19,300
My inner drill sergeant would like to see more rigor in study and more rigor in the instruction of Merit Badges,

95
00:07:19,560 --> 00:07:22,960
but understanding what Merit Badges represent, what they're supposed to represent,

96
00:07:22,960 --> 00:07:28,979
and how Scouts will have learned a little something about a given field of study or endeavor.

97
00:07:29,460 --> 00:07:32,299
I'm quite satisfied with the way that the system works.

98
00:07:32,680 --> 00:07:39,340
I will have a little internal dialogue with the internal drill sergeant, and I will say, you know, it's not that big a deal.

99
00:07:39,900 --> 00:07:43,539
Baden Powell said something about the jolity of Scouting.

100
00:07:44,099 --> 00:07:50,560
Jolity is not a word that you hear a whole lot, but, you know, Baden Powell was born in 1857, so let's cut him a break.

101
00:07:50,560 --> 00:07:59,920
That indicates to me a certain amount of lightness and fun and intensity and excitement surrounding the work of Scouting.

102
00:08:00,340 --> 00:08:02,480
I wouldn't be too upset about the Merit Badges days.

103
00:08:02,580 --> 00:08:03,180
I kind of agree.

104
00:08:03,300 --> 00:08:04,640
I'm a little suspicious of the whole thing.

105
00:08:04,720 --> 00:08:10,680
I'm suspicious of, you know, 30 guys and a 16-year-old counselor trying to complete swimming Merit Badget camp.

106
00:08:10,980 --> 00:08:15,200
Just how rigorous and complete can the instruction be in that kind of a situation?

107
00:08:15,200 --> 00:08:21,120
But it's been going on for years, and, you know, I'm satisfied that the guys have gotten out of it what they need to get out of it.

108
00:08:21,520 --> 00:08:23,000
These are not my badges to protect.

109
00:08:23,380 --> 00:08:25,020
They're not my standards to protect.

110
00:08:25,479 --> 00:08:29,620
I have yet to embroider, hand-embroider a Merit Badge and hand it out at a court of honor.

111
00:08:30,120 --> 00:08:36,240
We go and buy them from the Boy Scouts of America who own the Merit Badge and who own the program.

112
00:08:36,240 --> 00:08:45,080
And I will assent to the way that they're going to administer it, even though the inner drill sergeant may have some difficulties with it.

113
00:08:45,100 --> 00:08:46,220
So I hope that helps you, Mark.

114
00:08:46,700 --> 00:08:50,400
And if you need more clarification on that, do drop me a line.

115
00:08:52,380 --> 00:09:00,180
This has to be the truth, folks, because there is no way anyone could make this up.

116
00:09:00,180 --> 00:09:11,199
So I've been a Scoutmaster long enough that my career stretches back to the Beavis and Butthead age.

117
00:09:12,640 --> 00:09:21,640
If you don't know what I'm referring to when I speak of Beavis and Butthead, consider yourself lucky and move on.

118
00:09:21,640 --> 00:09:23,640
Thank you.

119
00:09:30,180 --> 00:09:38,020
Beavis and Butthead were characters in a cartoon show.

120
00:09:39,060 --> 00:09:44,160
They were your quintessential teenage kind of dopey kids.

121
00:09:44,719 --> 00:09:52,020
And they had catchphrases and spoke in this kind of adolescent code.

122
00:09:52,719 --> 00:09:56,599
And I never really cared for them, personally.

123
00:09:56,600 --> 00:10:00,800
I mean, I see, you know, it was Mike Judge and the guys.

124
00:10:01,020 --> 00:10:05,660
And they were, of course, very good writers and everything like that.

125
00:10:05,760 --> 00:10:08,760
But Beavis and Butthead, for one reason or another, just drove me crazy.

126
00:10:09,540 --> 00:10:18,500
Now, it's important to realize that it's the job of each succeeding generation to drive the older folks a little nutty, right?

127
00:10:19,120 --> 00:10:20,520
You did it and I did it.

128
00:10:20,620 --> 00:10:24,300
So I'm not making a blanket condemnation of anybody here.

129
00:10:24,300 --> 00:10:29,300
But I had a couple of guys in the scout troop and God bless them.

130
00:10:29,900 --> 00:10:35,000
They were the biggest Beavis and Butthead fans you would ever want to be.

131
00:10:35,000 --> 00:10:51,560
And they assumed the characters of Beavis and Butthead.

132
00:10:52,140 --> 00:10:56,200
And they just were pretty much 24-7.

133
00:10:57,000 --> 00:10:59,060
They did not break character.

134
00:10:59,320 --> 00:11:00,240
They spoke like them.

135
00:11:00,320 --> 00:11:01,200
They acted like them.

136
00:11:01,200 --> 00:11:10,160
And it just got so very tiresome to hear all little catchphrases and the attitude and things like that.

137
00:11:10,820 --> 00:11:15,320
Well, we struggled with that for a couple of years.

138
00:11:15,740 --> 00:11:21,820
And, you know, the worst thing you can do with something like that is draw attention to it by telling them to stop.

139
00:11:21,820 --> 00:11:25,680
You know, they have to live their own lives.

140
00:11:26,040 --> 00:11:28,640
It wasn't improper, but it was just aggregating.

141
00:11:29,200 --> 00:11:30,580
But I'll tell you something.

142
00:11:30,960 --> 00:11:35,400
I found a secret and I found out how to make them stop forever and ever.

143
00:11:35,400 --> 00:11:52,540
We were on a backpacking trip.

144
00:11:52,540 --> 00:11:55,680
And these guys were doing the old Beavis and Butthead thing.

145
00:11:56,040 --> 00:11:59,620
And I had a younger assistant scoutmaster along with me.

146
00:11:59,620 --> 00:12:07,280
And we were sitting there kind of, you know, saying, oh, they're at it again.

147
00:12:07,760 --> 00:12:10,640
They're doing these voices and they're using all the catchphrases.

148
00:12:11,040 --> 00:12:12,740
You know, I'm pretty sick of this.

149
00:12:13,320 --> 00:12:22,140
And for whatever reason, we just decided to start to adopt the Beavis and Butthead attitude and the catchphrases and the sayings ourselves.

150
00:12:22,140 --> 00:12:24,000
And it worked like a charm.

151
00:12:24,720 --> 00:12:37,980
Because as soon as myself and my assistant scoutmaster started using these catchphrases and adopting the attitude and everything like that, the scouts dropped it like a hot potato.

152
00:12:37,980 --> 00:13:02,100
Oh, I can remember very clearly the look of utter disgust and dismay that I received from the Beavis and Butthead adherents, the two of them,

153
00:13:02,100 --> 00:13:09,640
when we started using their holy phrases and attitude and the tone of voice.

154
00:13:11,460 --> 00:13:13,800
It's a good way to stop things like that.

155
00:13:14,140 --> 00:13:17,980
Just adopt it and say that it's the coolest thing you ever heard.

156
00:13:18,560 --> 00:13:22,200
And the boys will drop it and never go back.

157
00:13:22,400 --> 00:13:23,300
Never, ever.

158
00:13:23,520 --> 00:13:24,140
And they didn't.

159
00:13:24,400 --> 00:13:28,940
For the entire time they were in the truth, they never went back and did the Beavis and Butthead thing.

160
00:13:32,100 --> 00:13:40,240
My father was a wandering man.

161
00:13:40,460 --> 00:13:42,240
And it's not a good thing.

162
00:13:43,560 --> 00:13:47,480
I'm going to walk around, so long I can.

163
00:13:47,720 --> 00:13:50,240
And I'm going to walk around.

164
00:13:51,240 --> 00:13:52,300
All the way.

165
00:13:53,040 --> 00:13:54,400
All the way.

166
00:13:54,840 --> 00:13:55,720
All the way.

167
00:13:56,720 --> 00:13:57,340
All the way.

168
00:13:57,340 --> 00:13:59,340
All the way.

169
00:13:59,340 --> 00:13:59,660
All the way.

170
00:13:59,660 --> 00:14:01,660
All the way.

171
00:14:02,100 --> 00:14:03,520
All the way.

172
00:14:23,480 --> 00:14:26,880
All the way.

173
00:14:26,880 --> 00:14:42,380
This is installment number four in our podcast feature, Instructional Methods for Scouts.

174
00:14:42,380 --> 00:14:57,380
I'll remind you one more time that you can get all of this information on a PDF at scoutmaster.typepad.com.

175
00:14:57,460 --> 00:15:04,280
One of the methods that I find pretty useful in instructing scouts is called the coach and pupil method.

176
00:15:04,580 --> 00:15:08,400
Some people will call this like each one teach one.

177
00:15:08,400 --> 00:15:14,319
I think we have now the edge method that's talked about in the way of instruction.

178
00:15:14,459 --> 00:15:23,680
But the coach and pupil method is basically something that I have lifted from the National Rifle Association

179
00:15:23,680 --> 00:15:31,180
because I served for several summers as the field sports director at our scout camp,

180
00:15:31,579 --> 00:15:35,240
which means I ran the rifle range and the shotgun range

181
00:15:35,240 --> 00:15:39,140
and was responsible for the fellow who was working on the archery range.

182
00:15:39,400 --> 00:15:43,880
And we used this coach and pupil method all the time because it was something that was taught to us

183
00:15:43,880 --> 00:15:48,200
in gaining the certification that we needed to run these rifle ranges.

184
00:15:49,080 --> 00:15:52,920
And I found it really, really useful.

185
00:15:53,420 --> 00:15:57,440
So shooting a rifle or shooting a shotgun or shooting archery,

186
00:15:57,800 --> 00:16:00,920
there's about eight different things to think about for each shot.

187
00:16:00,920 --> 00:16:08,540
And what we would do with these is we would instruct all the scouts in them,

188
00:16:09,040 --> 00:16:12,219
in the eight different things as we demonstrated them.

189
00:16:12,459 --> 00:16:14,500
And then we would put them into pairs and say,

190
00:16:14,640 --> 00:16:18,500
okay, so now you're going to go ahead and one of you is going to be the coach

191
00:16:18,500 --> 00:16:20,680
and one of you is going to be the pupil.

192
00:16:21,360 --> 00:16:25,959
And what's going to happen is the pupil is going to move through these eight different steps

193
00:16:25,960 --> 00:16:30,420
of getting off a good shot and the coach is going to watch.

194
00:16:30,840 --> 00:16:35,420
And the coach may have a merit badge book or, you know, a list.

195
00:16:35,880 --> 00:16:38,880
I think we pretty much had this on a poster up on the wall.

196
00:16:39,320 --> 00:16:43,800
And it would be the coach's responsibility to make sure that the pupil took all eight steps

197
00:16:43,800 --> 00:16:46,400
and was mindful of them.

198
00:16:46,560 --> 00:16:48,080
And this was really, really useful.

199
00:16:48,420 --> 00:16:50,680
And then we would do that for two or three evolutions

200
00:16:50,680 --> 00:16:53,400
and then the coach and the pupil roles would change.

201
00:16:54,300 --> 00:16:58,239
And so watching somebody and coaching them through a skill

202
00:16:58,239 --> 00:17:02,359
is incredibly helpful in learning the skill yourself.

203
00:17:03,540 --> 00:17:05,940
You know, at the first go-around, you have no idea what you're doing.

204
00:17:06,359 --> 00:17:11,240
You know, the coach is not as effective as you will be when he's been the pupil

205
00:17:11,240 --> 00:17:13,419
and then he gets to be the coach again.

206
00:17:14,000 --> 00:17:17,379
So how do you use this in a scout troop?

207
00:17:17,380 --> 00:17:21,740
Well, for just about anything that is a manual skill.

208
00:17:22,380 --> 00:17:29,360
For knots, using a compass, orienting a map, pitching a tent, building a fire.

209
00:17:30,200 --> 00:17:36,120
Just I can, you know, apply this to almost any skill that's taught in scouts.

210
00:17:36,460 --> 00:17:41,540
And a couple of, you know, other things like more amorphous things

211
00:17:41,540 --> 00:17:43,560
like memorizing the scout oath and law

212
00:17:43,560 --> 00:17:48,980
and being able to say them from memory, you know,

213
00:17:48,980 --> 00:17:52,820
or any set of facts, you know, like for wilderness survival

214
00:17:52,820 --> 00:17:55,960
or a merit badge or something like that.

215
00:17:56,020 --> 00:17:58,500
This coach and pupil method really works well.

216
00:17:59,040 --> 00:18:04,740
So that's another excellent method to apply to scout instruction.

217
00:18:04,740 --> 00:18:11,940
One of the other ones that I use less frequently but I think is also very, very helpful

218
00:18:11,940 --> 00:18:14,040
is using Kim's game.

219
00:18:14,640 --> 00:18:19,020
Now, do you remember Rudyard Kipling's book, Kim?

220
00:18:19,880 --> 00:18:20,860
Maybe you didn't read it.

221
00:18:21,320 --> 00:18:26,440
But Kim's the story of an orphan son of an Irish soldier in India.

222
00:18:26,440 --> 00:18:30,800
And he was trained for government intelligence work

223
00:18:30,800 --> 00:18:33,980
by showing him a tray of precious stones for a minute

224
00:18:33,980 --> 00:18:38,000
and then covering it up and asking him to name all the stones

225
00:18:38,000 --> 00:18:39,040
and what kind they were.

226
00:18:39,780 --> 00:18:43,140
And this is the way he developed his skills of observation and memory.

227
00:18:43,360 --> 00:18:46,920
And this was one of the things that was adapted out of Kipling

228
00:18:46,920 --> 00:18:49,040
and into scouts by Baden-Powell.

229
00:18:49,420 --> 00:18:50,840
And we call it Kim's game.

230
00:18:51,740 --> 00:18:53,740
So now you know where it came from.

231
00:18:53,740 --> 00:18:58,060
And commonly the game's played with, you know,

232
00:18:58,140 --> 00:19:01,640
about anywhere from one to two dozen articles of different kinds.

233
00:19:01,900 --> 00:19:03,220
They're placed on a table.

234
00:19:03,360 --> 00:19:04,680
The table's covered with a cloth.

235
00:19:04,780 --> 00:19:06,860
The cloth is removed for about a minute

236
00:19:06,860 --> 00:19:11,960
while the players look on and try and remember

237
00:19:11,960 --> 00:19:15,020
all the articles underneath the cloth.

238
00:19:15,260 --> 00:19:20,000
The cloth is replaced and then usually the player or the group

239
00:19:20,000 --> 00:19:22,160
writes down what articles they remember

240
00:19:22,160 --> 00:19:25,060
and they're tested against what is found under the cloth.

241
00:19:25,440 --> 00:19:28,180
Now this is a really terrific instructional method.

242
00:19:28,700 --> 00:19:29,440
It really is.

243
00:19:29,440 --> 00:19:34,940
So let's say we are talking about the rank requirement

244
00:19:34,940 --> 00:19:39,100
where guys need to identify a number of plants.

245
00:19:39,600 --> 00:19:43,860
So we get either pictures or actual samples of the plants

246
00:19:43,860 --> 00:19:45,460
and we put them under the blanket

247
00:19:45,460 --> 00:19:49,620
and we show them to the guys for a minute

248
00:19:49,620 --> 00:19:51,640
and then they need to come back

249
00:19:51,640 --> 00:19:57,220
and tell us which ones they saw and they recognized.

250
00:19:57,880 --> 00:19:58,000
Okay?

251
00:19:58,560 --> 00:20:01,340
And then, you know, we can play this so many different ways.

252
00:20:01,460 --> 00:20:03,800
We can put the names on the pictures

253
00:20:03,800 --> 00:20:05,700
or next to the samples

254
00:20:05,700 --> 00:20:11,200
or we can just do the samples or pictures without names.

255
00:20:11,200 --> 00:20:14,400
We can use the scientific names of the samples.

256
00:20:14,980 --> 00:20:16,980
And this now becomes a game

257
00:20:16,980 --> 00:20:21,760
and it's not so much of a slogging kind of a thing.

258
00:20:22,480 --> 00:20:25,460
And this really excites the guy's curiosity and interest.

259
00:20:26,320 --> 00:20:28,960
You can do this with so many different things.

260
00:20:29,680 --> 00:20:33,580
With the symbols on a topographic map.

261
00:20:33,580 --> 00:20:37,760
You can just draw them on a 3x5 card

262
00:20:37,760 --> 00:20:40,919
and put a number of 3x5 cards under the blanket.

263
00:20:41,460 --> 00:20:43,280
And again, you can put the name on them

264
00:20:43,280 --> 00:20:44,379
or not have the name on them.

265
00:20:44,560 --> 00:20:46,740
You know, you can do this in progressing

266
00:20:46,740 --> 00:20:51,379
from easier ways of memorizing

267
00:20:51,379 --> 00:20:53,620
to more difficult ways of memorizing things.

268
00:20:54,280 --> 00:20:59,699
But it does make an interesting game for the guys.

269
00:21:00,179 --> 00:21:02,620
Animal tracks, animal pictures, knots.

270
00:21:02,620 --> 00:21:04,479
First aid supplies.

271
00:21:04,939 --> 00:21:07,520
There's just an endless number of variations

272
00:21:07,520 --> 00:21:09,280
that you can use in this Kim's game.

273
00:21:10,219 --> 00:21:13,800
Next time around will be the final installment in this series.

274
