And we'll be talking about

276
00:21:16,879 --> 00:21:18,760
how to prepare for instruction

277
00:21:18,760 --> 00:21:23,120
and who should be instructing in your scout troop.

278
00:21:23,120 --> 00:21:49,659
That's right.

279
00:21:49,660 --> 00:21:52,880
It's time for a Scoutmaster's Minute.

280
00:22:01,040 --> 00:22:03,220
A scout is trustworthy.

281
00:22:04,080 --> 00:22:05,240
A scout tells the truth.

282
00:22:05,340 --> 00:22:05,880
He's honest.

283
00:22:06,240 --> 00:22:07,100
He keeps his promises.

284
00:22:07,520 --> 00:22:08,640
He can be dependent upon.

285
00:22:10,300 --> 00:22:11,700
Ralph Waldo Emerson said,

286
00:22:11,700 --> 00:22:15,920
Trust men and they will be true to you.

287
00:22:16,420 --> 00:22:19,080
Treat them greatly and they will show themselves great.

288
00:22:19,720 --> 00:22:23,140
Trustworthiness is a reciprocal kind of a virtue.

289
00:22:24,520 --> 00:22:29,640
A stable life hinges on unconditional trust in a lot of things.

290
00:22:29,640 --> 00:22:33,800
As a minor, as a bus schedule,

291
00:22:34,420 --> 00:22:38,660
or as significant as the daily path of the sun through the sky,

292
00:22:38,860 --> 00:22:41,640
we trust the predictability of things and events.

293
00:22:41,640 --> 00:22:45,400
A trustworthy person is dependable,

294
00:22:45,800 --> 00:22:47,740
but not merely predictable.

295
00:22:47,740 --> 00:22:52,780
We trust people who have consistent ethical judgment,

296
00:22:53,480 --> 00:22:56,880
whose actions spring from intellectual integrity.

297
00:22:57,900 --> 00:23:00,860
Trustworthy people are less concerned with appearing to be honorable

298
00:23:00,860 --> 00:23:04,360
than actually being honorable and doing the honorable thing.

299
00:23:05,220 --> 00:23:08,000
They don't compromise ethics under pressure.

300
00:23:09,280 --> 00:23:11,780
A trustworthy friend is a real treasure.

301
00:23:11,780 --> 00:23:14,800
They're both faithful and honest,

302
00:23:15,500 --> 00:23:18,340
and they seek respect before approval,

303
00:23:18,780 --> 00:23:23,139
and win confidence through sincerity, not flattery.

304
00:23:24,220 --> 00:23:27,680
Trusting others tends to engender their trust, as Emerson said.

305
00:23:28,540 --> 00:23:30,520
When we're honest and dependable,

306
00:23:30,680 --> 00:23:33,360
it's much more likely that others are going to be the same.

307
00:23:34,280 --> 00:23:36,680
And I think we see it a lot in scouts.

308
00:23:37,120 --> 00:23:39,360
We see the reciprocation of trust.

309
00:23:39,860 --> 00:23:41,520
When we trust the scouts,

310
00:23:41,780 --> 00:23:42,700
they trust us.

311
00:23:42,700 --> 00:23:47,720
That kind of trust circle is something that's hard to break,

312
00:23:48,120 --> 00:23:51,320
because a scout is trustworthy.

